WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.600
If you’re a Kevin Smith fan, you may already&nbsp;
be familiar with Jay and Silent Bob’s cameo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:07.120
in Scream 3, but you might not know the&nbsp;
backstory behind it. Jay and Silent Bob are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:11.200
a pair of characters that first appeared in Kevin&nbsp;
Smith’s 1994 movie, Clerks, which is prominently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:14.720
displayed at the video store in the original&nbsp;
Scream. Jay and Bob would become recurring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.720 --> 00:00:18.400
characters who show up in many Kevin Smith&nbsp;
films that are part of his View Askewniverse,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.400 --> 00:00:22.160
and as a thank you for the free advertising&nbsp;
in Scream, Kevin Smith and Scream director Wes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:27.200
Craven decided to exchange cameos in each other’s&nbsp;
movies. So, Jay and Silent Bob show up in Scream 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.200
and the following year, Wes shows up in Jay&nbsp;
and Silent Bob Strike Back, where he plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:35.760
Wes Craven the director of Scream 4, a decade&nbsp;
before Wes would actually create Scream 4.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.400
In addition to that, Cotton Weary’s&nbsp;
apartment in the opening of Scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.320
3 contains a copy of Chasing Amy, which&nbsp;
is also part of the View Askewniverse.

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:46.400
The scene with Jay and Silent Bob also contains&nbsp;
a couple other cameos, which are pretty much only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.400 --> 00:00:50.800
findable thanks to the retrospective documentary&nbsp;
Still Screaming, which points out a cameo from Wes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:55.440
Craven as the man with a video camera, and Scream&nbsp;
3’s casting director Lisa Beach as the tour guide.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.680
To learn how Scream 3 may have had a second&nbsp;
killer who died before being revealed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:01.600
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:01.932 --> 00:01:04.282
(metal music)

00:01:08.080 --> 00:01:12.240
In Scream, movies don’t create psychos,&nbsp;
movies make psychos more creative.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:17.120
In Scream 2, movies are a scapegoat for someone&nbsp;
who is planning to be a killer. Scream 3 reverses&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:20.960
this and has the killer make the movie,&nbsp;
creating his own lore every step of the way.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:24.000
Welcome to Things You Missed. Wanting&nbsp;
to capitalize on the hype train created&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:28.000
by Scream 1 and 2, producers decided to go&nbsp;
ahead without screenwriter Kevin Williamson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:31.040
by replacing him with Ehren Kruger.&nbsp;
Really? The creator of A Nightmare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.040 --> 00:01:33.040
On Elm Street is working with a guy named Kruger?

00:01:33.040 --> 00:01:34.245
– [Jennifer] Stage name.

00:01:34.245 --> 00:01:36.400
– You should talk, Judy Jurgenstern.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.280
– [Group] OHHHHH!!!!!

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:39.760
– Sidney Prescott has become&nbsp;
a crisis hotline counselor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.760 --> 00:01:43.360
and she also takes on a stage name of&nbsp;
sorts, going by Laura to her callers.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.960
She’s unknowingly following in the footsteps&nbsp;
of her mother, who used the name Rina Reynolds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.960 --> 00:01:50.480
while working in Hollywood. There’s a&nbsp;
running theme of pseudonyms in Scream 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.480 --> 00:01:53.680
Most of our main characters are actors&nbsp;
who are just as likely to respond to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:57.400
their character names as their actual names,&nbsp;
to the point that it becomes kind of a joke.

00:01:58.415 --> 00:02:02.480
– Not “Sidney”, Sidney. Like&nbsp;
I’m “Gale” and she’s Gale.

00:02:02.480 --> 00:02:05.280
– Obviously, it’s more than just a joke&nbsp;
though, it’s important to the plot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:07.600
because Roman’s changing of&nbsp;
his last name to Bridger is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:11.280
what helps disguise his true identity&nbsp;
as Sidney’s brother and the killer.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:13.231
Let’s get into the Things You Missed.

00:02:13.231 --> 00:02:16.697
(brooding music)
(knives slashing)

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:22.320
As with Screams 1 and 2, the opening shows&nbsp;
the slaying of a couple, only this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.320 --> 00:02:25.760
we recognize one of the victims, it’s&nbsp;
Cotton Weary and his girlfriend. Cotton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:30.160
is complaining that he is only given a cameo&nbsp;
in Stab 3, which is basically his experience in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.160 --> 00:02:34.160
Scream 3 -- despite being a big part of the second&nbsp;
movie, they kill him off right away in this one.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.840
One the criticisms of turning Scream into a&nbsp;
franchise was that it would become the very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:42.400
thing that the original Scream was satirizing, and&nbsp;
we see this intentionally, or not intentionally in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.400 --> 00:02:46.880
Scream 3’s opening. In the last film, Stab 1&nbsp;
attempted to recreate the opening of Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:49.920
the death of Casey Becker. But certain&nbsp;
things are embellished for Hollywood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:53.840
like how they add a shower scene which seems to&nbsp;
have no purpose other than showing a little skin.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:57.440
So Scream 3’s opening literally does that,&nbsp;
in the form of Cotton’s girlfriend getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.440 --> 00:03:00.720
out of the shower. There’s even a shot of&nbsp;
Christine’s towel dropping by her feet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:02.720
very similar to Casey’s bathrobe in Stab.

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:06.000
– The whole shower thing’s&nbsp;
been done! Vertigo. Hello?

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:10.160
– It’s not Vertigo, it’s Psycho. As we have&nbsp;
discussed many times on this show. Then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.160
Then, Christine even channels her inner Casey Becker&nbsp;
herself when she kicks Ghostface to get away.

00:03:14.160 --> 00:03:18.240
Scream 2’s opening was all about the Stab movie&nbsp;
premiere, so there’s a slight nod to that as well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:22.640
when Cotton is frantically driving home, he nearly&nbsp;
hits a bus with a Stab 2 advertisement on back.

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.160
This next one might not be a reference,&nbsp;
this might just be Cotton being entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.160 --> 00:03:30.080
focused on saving his girlfriend, but he&nbsp;
leaves his car door open as he goes inside,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.080 --> 00:03:34.160
much like Bob Simms in Halloween, who&nbsp;
was also probably focused on one thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:38.000
entirely. As you know if you’ve seen Scream,&nbsp;
Halloween is basically considered the big one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:41.840
and is referenced all the time. But not that&nbsp;
much in Scream 3. Although there is the one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:41.840 --> 00:03:45.120
part where Sidney hears her mother’s voice&nbsp;
telling her she’ll protect her from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:48.560
Boogeyman. The Boogeyman is a pseudonym&nbsp;
for Michael Myers in the Halloween films.

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.880
Ghostface tries to pressure Cotton into revealing&nbsp;
the location of Sidney Prescott before offing him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:56.560
and then we see Sidney living a secluded&nbsp;
life with her dog. By her door there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.560 --> 00:04:00.320
a Fall of Troy poster advertising the&nbsp;
play she was in in college, Agamemnon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.320 --> 00:04:02.400
Does that mean she actually went&nbsp;
through with being in the play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.680
after defeating Mickey and Debbie&nbsp;
on it’s stage? That’s dedication.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:09.200
She also continues to wear the Greek letters&nbsp;
given to her by her boyfriend when the murders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:12.640
were happening in the last movie. Can you really&nbsp;
blame her? He was the best guy she ever dated.&nbsp;

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.480
The next character we meet is Gale Weathers,&nbsp;
who is giving a speech about being a journalist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.480 --> 00:04:21.120
lady. A woman in the crowd is wearing a #81&nbsp;
jersey. Not many people know this, but Scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:27.280
1996 was not the first… or last… slasher movie to&nbsp;
be called Scream. The original was a 1981 film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.280 --> 00:04:30.800
I have not seen it, but according to the&nbsp;
Letterboxd ratings, it is not very good.

00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:34.720
This scene also has the best extra. This&nbsp;
lady is REALLY into Gale’s speech. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:37.840
speech is part of a lecture series&nbsp;
called FW Bestor Lecture Series:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.840 --> 00:04:41.600
Faces of Journalism, named after the movie’s&nbsp;
art department coordinator Claudia Bestor.

00:04:41.600 --> 00:04:43.920
Just like the last two movies, Sidney is at home,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.920 --> 00:04:47.360
when she sees bad news on the TV&nbsp;
that involves her and her past.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:50.960
As we’re first introduced to Sunrise Studios,&nbsp;
where most of this movie takes place, we hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.960 --> 00:04:54.960
the song Red Right Hand by Nick Cave and the Bad&nbsp;
Seeds, which by this point is more or less the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.960 --> 00:04:58.800
Scream theme song, this is our fourth time hearing&nbsp;
it, and the end credits would be the fifth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.800 --> 00:05:02.400
although that one is a special Scream 3&nbsp;
version meant to close out the trilogy.

00:05:02.400 --> 00:05:06.560
Stab 3’s subtitle is “Return to Woodsboro”, and&nbsp;
this phrase keeps finding new meaning throughout&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.400
the movie. It’s actually a leftover from Kevin&nbsp;
Williamson’s original outline for the script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:14.400
that he planned after writing Scream 2, but most&nbsp;
of it was not used for what would become Scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:18.080
3. We would symbolically return to Woodsboro&nbsp;
immediately, because they’re shooting on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.080 --> 00:05:22.720
Woodsboro set. Sidney would return to Woodsboro&nbsp;
when she is later chased through this set.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.720 --> 00:05:26.560
Later still, the focus of the story is brought&nbsp;
back to Woodsboro when we learn new information&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.560 --> 00:05:30.960
about the motivations of Billy and Stu. And the&nbsp;
movie would end with everyone returning Woodsboro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.480
or at least what appears to be Woodsboro&nbsp;
to join Sidney at her secluded home.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:38.720
But this first scene on the Woodsboro set&nbsp;
contains more depth than what meets the eye.

00:05:38.720 --> 00:05:44.074
(cinematic impact)

00:05:44.074 --> 00:05:47.241
(mysterious music)

00:05:47.918 --> 00:05:49.920
When we meet Roman, the director of Stab 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.920 --> 00:05:53.520
he’s busy making a case for keeping the&nbsp;
production going after Cotton’s death.

00:05:53.520 --> 00:05:56.720
– [Roman] Hire more security, hire the&nbsp;
national guard, just don’t kill the movie.

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:00.400
– Which is funny, because he literally would&nbsp;
kill the movie, at least for the time being,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:04.800
by killing everyone in it. He’s seen arguing&nbsp;
with a studio executive played by Roger Corman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.280
who tells him that violence in cinema is kind of&nbsp;
a no-no right now. Which is also a little joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.280 --> 00:06:12.560
because Roger Corman is a well known&nbsp;
horror producer and director. He literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.560 --> 00:06:16.800
made movies such as Night of the Blood&nbsp;
Beast. A Bucket of Blood. Bloody Mama.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:20.480
Battle of Blood Island. Blood Bath.&nbsp;
A remake of A Bucket of Blood.

00:06:20.480 --> 00:06:25.280
But his concern over gory movies in 1999&nbsp;
was very relevant, because Stab 3 is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:29.600
in-universe stand-in for Scream 3, and Scream&nbsp;
3 was going through the same problems at this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.200
time thanks to the Columbine Massacre, the&nbsp;
worst high school shooting of the millenium.

00:06:33.200 --> 00:06:38.000
The Columbine Massacre was carried out by two high&nbsp;
school seniors, and that is really only similarity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:42.000
to Scream that exists, but that didn’t stop&nbsp;
people who were already against horror movies.

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:47.280
– Everybody that didn’t like horror films that was&nbsp;
in a position of power, came after the studios.

00:06:47.280 --> 00:06:51.200
– [Zac] Columbine happened in April 1999.&nbsp;
Scream 3 started shooting in July,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:54.880
so there was a lot of pressure from the studio&nbsp;
to limit the gore, which is why we see less of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:59.200
it in this movie. According to Matthew Lillard,&nbsp;
he was already paid to appear in Scream 3 as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.200 --> 00:07:04.160
returning villain Stu Macher, but Columbine put&nbsp;
an end to those plans. Wes Craven attempted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.160 --> 00:07:07.680
make sure the cultural context was not&nbsp;
forgotten, by sneaking in this article&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:07.680 --> 00:07:11.200
about Columbine into the scene where Sidney&nbsp;
looks through her file at the police station.

00:07:11.200 --> 00:07:15.360
The other articles in this file are just nonsense.&nbsp;
Look at this one. “The local police chief,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:18.080
the first official commissioned by&nbsp;
the city with the search committed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:18.080 --> 00:07:22.720
in the name of the parents. The shadowy&nbsp;
suspect, faces 121 charges of murder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.720 --> 00:07:26.960
kidnapping, arms, smuggling, fraud&nbsp;
and theft. 'His plea is he hadn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.120
committed these offenses.' said the parents’&nbsp;
attorney, 'we will find the Megan at all cost.'"

00:07:31.120 --> 00:07:33.760
When Gale, that is, the&nbsp;
real Gale, shows up on set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.760 --> 00:07:36.414
she’s hit by that wave of dreadful familiarity.

00:07:37.892 --> 00:07:39.892
(creepy music)

00:07:41.292 --> 00:07:42.480
– Deja Voodoo.

00:07:42.480 --> 00:07:45.440
– Her misuse of the term déjà vu,&nbsp;
right outside of Sidney’s house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.440 --> 00:07:50.240
calls back to Sidney’s own misuse of the phrase in&nbsp;
Scream 1. The nostalgia does not end there though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:53.840
because the new Gale, aka Jennifer,&nbsp;
is dressed more like the old Gale.

00:07:53.840 --> 00:07:57.280
But what happens next is not all old&nbsp;
and familiar. Gale runs into Dewey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:57.280 --> 00:08:00.400
who has become an advisor for the&nbsp;
movie. When these two met in Scream 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.400 --> 00:08:03.200
Dewey was upset with how Gale&nbsp;
portrayed him in her book. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.200 --> 00:08:07.840
Dewey has turned the tables on her, and Gale is&nbsp;
upset about how he’s portraying her in Stab 3.

00:08:07.840 --> 00:08:11.440
In my previous Scream analysis videos, I&nbsp;
pointed out Wes Craven’s easter eggs to his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.440 --> 00:08:15.040
other major horror franchise, A Nightmare on&nbsp;
Elm Street. We don’t really get that much in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.040 --> 00:08:18.000
the third one, but there is this one&nbsp;
tiny nightmare scene involving Sidney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:19.760
where she sees her dead mother come to the window.

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:23.040
If we’re being honest though, the reference&nbsp;
points a lot more towards Salem’s Lot than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:25.920
it does towards Freddy Krueger.
The next day, Sarah Darling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:29.760
who plays the character Candy in Stab, has a&nbsp;
meeting with Roman at the production office.

00:08:29.760 --> 00:08:33.600
– I’m Candy, the chick who gets&nbsp;
killed second I’m only in two scenes.

00:08:33.600 --> 00:08:36.960
– Like Cotton’s gripes about his character,&nbsp;
the complaint about her screen time could&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:42.160
also apply to her role in Scream 3; she’s only in&nbsp;
2 scenes. There’s also another 1:1 parallel later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.160 --> 00:08:45.520
where the police mention that there are three&nbsp;
versions of the Stab 3 script, in order to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.520 --> 00:08:49.600
try to keep the ending off the internet -- the&nbsp;
exact same approach used for Scream 3. And then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:53.680
there’s the romance between Dewey and Gale.&nbsp;
This time, rather than art imitating life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:57.760
life would imitate art. These two got married&nbsp;
for realsies right before they started shooting.

00:08:57.760 --> 00:09:01.760
Anyway, during the line reading, Roman goes off&nbsp;
the script, and then claims there’s a new version.

00:09:01.760 --> 00:09:04.240
– It’s not just a new script, it’s a new movie.&nbsp;

00:09:04.240 --> 00:09:06.480
– What movie?
– My movie.

00:09:06.480 --> 00:09:08.960
– Although we don’t know it yet,&nbsp;
she’s speaking to the real Roman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.960 --> 00:09:12.240
and he’s telling the truth. By&nbsp;
killing the people involved in Stab 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:15.440
he’s creating the lore for Stab&nbsp;
3. He’s creating his movie.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:19.920
Candy is the successor to Tatum in Scream, and&nbsp;
her death closely resembles Tatum’s. Ghostface&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.920 --> 00:09:23.680
chases her into the prop room where, among&nbsp;
all the movie props, is a fridge. Remember,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.680 --> 00:09:26.560
Tatum went to the garage to get drinks&nbsp;
from the fridge. She’s punched through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.560 --> 00:09:28.800
a door where she’s left hanging&nbsp;
on her stomach, and this is where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:32.960
Ghostface get her. She dies in the exact same&nbsp;
position that Tatum died in in the pet flap.

00:09:32.960 --> 00:09:35.760
While Gale and Dewey are out to lunch&nbsp;
to discuss their past relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.760 --> 00:09:39.760
Gale emphasizes the importance of leaving&nbsp;
him alone to go on the sequel to 60 minutes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.760 --> 00:09:43.360
claiming she could have been the next Diane&nbsp;
Sawyer. It’s a bit of a Scream 2 reference,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.360 --> 00:09:47.200
Diane Sawyer was the journalist that Cotton&nbsp;
most wanted to be interviewed on to gain fame.

00:09:47.200 --> 00:09:50.720
Back on the movie set, we have maybe our&nbsp;
most obvious set of crew reference easter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.720 --> 00:09:55.040
eggs yet on the director’s chairs. Yes, they&nbsp;
are called that. This one’s for Peter Deming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:55.040 --> 00:09:57.920
the Director of Photography, this&nbsp;
one belongs to Sheila Waldron,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:01.840
the script supervisor and there’s one for&nbsp;
Bruce Alan Miller, the production designer.

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:05.360
That evening, the cast gathers for the&nbsp;
depressing wrap party at Jennifer’s house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.360 --> 00:10:08.880
where the killing continue with her&nbsp;
security guard, and Roman continues to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.880 --> 00:10:12.000
quote unquote write his movie by&nbsp;
literally sending in script pages&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.000 --> 00:10:15.920
telling them what to do via fax. Uhh, if&nbsp;
you don’t know what fax is, look it up.

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:19.120
The house blows up, taking&nbsp;
out Tom, aka: the new Dewey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.120 --> 00:10:22.720
and the old Gale punches the new Gale&nbsp;
in the face, this is yet another twist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.640
on the Scream tradition, of Ms. Weathers&nbsp;
getting a facefull of knuckles in each film.

00:10:26.640 --> 00:10:29.840
The characters end up at the police station&nbsp;
where they’re finally reunited with Sidney.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.840 --> 00:10:33.440
One nice detail at the station is the&nbsp;
display of movie posters and books about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:37.920
film. It’s like Randy suggested in Scream 1,&nbsp;
that the cops should study movies to save time.

00:10:37.920 --> 00:10:41.680
Speaking of Randy, he makes his return, in&nbsp;
the form of a post-mortem video message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.680 --> 00:10:45.528
which he would use to communicate&nbsp;
a few new horror movie rules.

00:10:45.528 --> 00:10:48.836
(cinematic impact)

00:10:52.080 --> 00:10:55.040
– [Randy] True trilogies are all about&nbsp;
going back to the beginning and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.040 --> 00:10:58.640
discovering something that wasn’t&nbsp;
true from the get-go. Godfather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.480
Jedi, all revealed something that we&nbsp;
thought was true, that wasn’t true.

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.840
– This was not his first time referencing&nbsp;
the original Star Wars trilogy, we saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.936 --> 00:11:09.204
him mention it in Scream 2, and it wouldn't be long before it came up again.

00:11:09.204 --> 00:11:12.681
The two Gales meet the Sunrise Studios archivist, who is played by&nbsp;Carrie Fisher,

00:11:12.681 --> 00:11:15.000
aka, Princess Leia in Star Wars.

00:11:15.364 --> 00:11:22.320
– I was up for Princess Leia. I was this close. So&nbsp;
who gets it? The one who sleeps with George Lucas.

00:11:22.320 --> 00:11:24.400
– She also shares something in common with Sidney,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.400 --> 00:11:28.720
her trilogy also involves the surprise twist&nbsp;
where she discovers someone is her brother.

00:11:28.720 --> 00:11:32.640
They pull out the file for Maureen Prescott,&nbsp;
who at the time was going by Rina Reynolds. At&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:36.000
the bottom of her resume, we can see that&nbsp;
she started her acting career in theatre,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.080
just as her daughter would go on to do. One of&nbsp;
the plays she was in was even called I Want To&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.080 --> 00:11:44.160
Scream. So they’ve got a whole little family&nbsp;
involved in the dramatic arts. Maureen with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:48.640
her acting in plays and horror films; Roman,&nbsp;
the director; and Sidney, the live performer.

00:11:48.640 --> 00:11:52.400
If Candy’s death was a callback to Tatum, this&nbsp;
scene where Sidney is chased through the movie set&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.400 --> 00:11:56.880
is a repeat of her own scene in Scream, right down&nbsp;
to her screwing herself by locking herself in with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.880 --> 00:12:00.880
Ghostface at the front door. There’s also another&nbsp;
similarity. In Scream, she calls the police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.880 --> 00:12:05.120
using this program on her computer, and Dewey&nbsp;
immediately shows up at her door. In Scream 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.120 --> 00:12:08.960
she literally just yells out for Dewey and he comes&nbsp;
running because he’s already at the studio.

00:12:08.960 --> 00:12:13.600
The cops show up and we see this one new cop named&nbsp;
“J West”. This is the last crew reference easter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.600 --> 00:12:16.960
egg, it’s a reference to the assistant&nbsp;
production coordinator John Burton West.

00:12:16.960 --> 00:12:18.960
After discovering Maureen worked in the movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:22.480
Dewey and the Gales confront the producer&nbsp;
John Milton, who basically tells them that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:26.240
he held these parties back in the day where&nbsp;
industry bigwigs took advantage of actresses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:26.240 --> 00:12:28.880
and he basically turned a blind&nbsp;
eye to this illegal activity.

00:12:28.880 --> 00:12:33.040
If you’ve followed the entertainment industry&nbsp;
recently, this might sound familiar. In 2018&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.040 --> 00:12:36.960
film producer Harvey Weinstein was arrested&nbsp;
as many allegations came out against him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.960 --> 00:12:41.120
some of which dated back to the 1970s,&nbsp;
the very same time period that John&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.120 --> 00:12:45.200
Milton claimed to have these parties.&nbsp;
Could this be a coincidence? Maybe not.

00:12:45.200 --> 00:12:48.480
From my understanding, Weinstein got away&nbsp;
with this for as long as he did because he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.480 --> 00:12:51.920
targeted small time actresses. They did&nbsp;
not take legal action because they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:56.320
afraid they’d be blacklisted from the industry,&nbsp;
since Harvey had contacts with all of Hollywood.

00:12:56.320 --> 00:12:59.440
For example, two actresses up for&nbsp;
parts in Lord of the Rings lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:59.440 --> 00:13:02.400
their roles simply because Weinstein&nbsp;
told Peter Jackson that they were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:02.400 --> 00:13:05.040
“nightmare to work with.” That’s&nbsp;
the kind of influence that he had.

00:13:05.040 --> 00:13:08.800
But with the advent of the internet and social&nbsp;
media, the victims were able to discover that they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:08.800 --> 00:13:13.600
were not the only ones. There were a lot. Like&nbsp;
a whole lot. So they came together, knowing that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.600 --> 00:13:17.520
they couldn’t ALL be blacklisted. The point of all&nbsp;
this is, while they didn’t get him arrested for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.520 --> 00:13:21.840
many years, there were rumors about Weinstein’s&nbsp;
behavior. So it’s very possible that Wes Craven&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.840 --> 00:13:25.840
may have known about these rumors and pulled&nbsp;
the biggest power move of all time, by putting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:25.840 --> 00:13:31.600
Harvey Weinstein character in his movie. Which by&nbsp;
the way was produced by Bob and Harvey Weinstein.

00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:35.120
But unlike these relatively unknown&nbsp;
actresses, Wes Craven had already made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.120 --> 00:13:38.560
it in Hollywood. Weinstein couldn’t&nbsp;
do anything to him, and besides,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:38.560 --> 00:13:42.320
he was already nearing the end of his career.&nbsp;
And despite being the executive producer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:42.320 --> 00:13:47.280
Harvey Weinstein couldn’t really stop Wes from&nbsp;
using Scream 3 to make this indirect accusation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:47.280 --> 00:13:51.120
Because if he order Wes to change the John&nbsp;
Milton scene, he’s just gonna look guilty.

00:13:51.120 --> 00:13:54.320
According to the film’s editor, Patrick&nbsp;
Lussier, in an article on Slate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.320 --> 00:13:58.160
the only note to come from the Weinstein&nbsp;
brothers at all, came from Harvey’s brother, Bob,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.160 --> 00:14:02.240
he just had a note about the ending, which I’ll&nbsp;
get back to later in this video. So Wes had his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:02.240 --> 00:14:06.080
boss backed into a corner where he could not&nbsp;
give any notes. It’s got to be one of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:06.080 --> 00:14:11.120
legendary moves that Wes has ever made if this,&nbsp;
in fact, is not a total, huge coincidence.

00:14:11.120 --> 00:14:13.840
– Hollywood is full of criminals&nbsp;
whose careers are flourishing.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:14.906
– I'm not a criminal!

00:14:15.040 --> 00:14:17.440
– Yeah, I don’t think it is. And to&nbsp;
further add to the suspicion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:21.200
some of the accusers are connected to the&nbsp;
Scream franchise. Rosanna Arquette is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:25.520
real life sister of David Arquette, Dewey.&nbsp;
And Rose McGowan, who plays Tatum in Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:25.520 --> 00:14:30.000
is the onscreen sister of David Arquette. Angelina&nbsp;
Jolie, potentially has two characters named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:34.000
after her in Scream 3, Jennifer Jolie&nbsp;
and Angelina Tyler who says this:

00:14:34.000 --> 00:14:37.520
– I did not f*ck that pig Milton&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:37.520 --> 00:14:42.720
to get a leading role just to die here&nbsp;
with second-rate celebrities like you two!

00:14:42.720 --> 00:14:46.640
– The movie’s climax takes place at a mansion party&nbsp;
where Roman’s birthday is to be celebrated. While&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:50.000
we don’t realize it at the time, I don’t&nbsp;
think it’s any coincidence that Roman puts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:50.000 --> 00:14:54.320
his plan into action on his birthday, the day&nbsp;
he became Maureen Prescott’s bastard child.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:58.160
The party splits up to explore the mansion&nbsp;
and Jennifer finds herself stuck in a passage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:01.840
with Ghostface. She sees Dewey on the other&nbsp;
side of this soundproof glass barrier and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.840 --> 00:15:06.080
struggles to get his attention. This is the same&nbsp;
situation that the real Gale was in in Scream 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:08.720
when she was unable to communicate&nbsp;
with him in the recording studio.

00:15:08.720 --> 00:15:12.000
There's another repeat of a Scream 2&nbsp;
situation in the basement when Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:12.000 --> 00:15:14.880
is knocked out after falling down&nbsp;
the stairs, but his body blocks the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:14.880 --> 00:15:18.880
only exit. This is like when Sidney had to&nbsp;
climb over Mickey to escape the crashed car.

00:15:18.880 --> 00:15:22.320
Roman uses his capture of Gale and&nbsp;
Dewey to lure Sidney into the house,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:26.080
but she's prepared and greets him with her&nbsp;
hidden gun. Unfortunately for her, this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.080 --> 00:15:30.400
the one time that she didn't deliver a follow up shot&nbsp;
to the head and it comes back back bite her.

00:15:30.400 --> 00:15:33.280
The final confrontation takes place&nbsp;
in the secret screening room where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:36.880
Roman was theoretically conceived. After&nbsp;
revealing himself as the killer there are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.880 --> 00:15:40.560
a couple more parallels to Scream 1, the&nbsp;
bringing out of a gagged victim and Roman&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:40.560 --> 00:15:44.400
cocking his head just like Ghostface when he&nbsp;
first pinned down Sidney at her dad’s house.

00:15:44.400 --> 00:15:48.000
After he is defeated, we’re treated to an&nbsp;
epilogue. We see Sidney essentially let&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.000 --> 00:15:51.440
her guard down for the first time since&nbsp;
that fateful phone call a few years ago.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:55.360
Then we see Detective Kincaid, the cop&nbsp;
from LA, who apparently came back with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:55.360 --> 00:15:58.080
her? He’s holding a bowl of&nbsp;
popcorn and says they’re about to start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:58.080 --> 00:16:02.560
a movie. So the Scream trilogy ends just&nbsp;
as it began, with popcorn and a movie.

00:16:02.560 --> 00:16:06.080
I personally think this was supposed to be&nbsp;
the end of the series. By comparing the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:06.080 --> 00:16:09.200
ever Scream scene to the first ever,&nbsp;
we see the changed state of the world,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:09.200 --> 00:16:13.040
before and after the defeat of Roman, the driving&nbsp;
force behind all of the bad things that happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:18.880
to Sidney in the 1990s. However, having only one&nbsp;
killer doesn’t follow the Scream formula. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.880 --> 00:16:23.520
could be one more villain from the production&nbsp;
of Stab 3 who has yet to be taken care of.

00:16:23.520 --> 00:16:27.922
(cinematic impact)

00:16:29.342 --> 00:16:31.342
(CONTAGIOUS spooky dance music)

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:37.760
Earlier in this video I mentioned two darker&nbsp;
topics, Weinstein and Columbine. I stated that Wes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.760 --> 00:16:41.920
Craven and his editor only received one note on&nbsp;
the film and it came from the innocent of the two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:46.480
brothers, Bob Weinstein. His idea was to remove&nbsp;
the second Ghostface to just focus on Roman.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:50.240
The reason for this, or at least one of the&nbsp;
reasons, may have been teased in the film.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:53.120
Remember the scene where the producers are&nbsp;
having a discussion with Roman about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.120 --> 00:16:56.560
future of the film, emphasizing that&nbsp;
stringed instruments in cinema are a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.560 --> 00:17:00.720
big deal right now? A big reason for that was&nbsp;
Columbine. So the shooting may have also been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.720 --> 00:17:04.640
part of the reason that they moved away from&nbsp;
the initial concept of having two killers.

00:17:04.640 --> 00:17:08.400
The accomplice was going to be Angelina Tyler,&nbsp;
the woman who was set to play the fictional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:08.400 --> 00:17:13.200
version of Sidney in Stab 3. You can still see&nbsp;
remnants of this storyline in the film. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:13.200 --> 00:17:16.720
some fans believe that Angelina is&nbsp;
still canonically the 6th Ghostface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:16.720 --> 00:17:19.600
and she was killed before she ever&nbsp;
got the chance to reveal herself.

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:21.760
Her story is that she won&nbsp;
the role in a talent search,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.760 --> 00:17:24.480
but we later find she did this&nbsp;
by sleeping with the producer.

00:17:24.480 --> 00:17:27.332
– [Tom] You gotta be praying this movie keeps going.

00:17:27.332 --> 00:17:28.585
– Of course I am, but not at the expense

00:17:28.585 --> 00:17:29.760
of people’s lives.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:31.840
– [Zac] You either die a hero or live --

00:17:32.152 --> 00:17:35.520
There are three main sequences that make Angelina&nbsp;
look guilty. The first is shortly after Jennifer’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:35.520 --> 00:17:39.280
house explodes. She briefly disappears, and&nbsp;
comes down the hill well after everyone else,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:39.280 --> 00:17:42.480
almost like she knew where to jump to&nbsp;
avoid taking fall damage. This allows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:46.080
her to completely miss the altercation with&nbsp;
Roman. Dewey asks her about her whereabouts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:46.080 --> 00:17:49.234
and she never actually answers the question.

00:17:49.234 --> 00:17:50.373
– [Angelina] Tom was in the house!

00:17:50.373 --> 00:17:54.160
Then, there’s the bathroom scene, which by the way&nbsp;
is setup exactly like the school bathroom scene in

00:17:54.160 --> 00:17:58.560
Scream 1. Sidney discovers her seemingly changing&nbsp;
into the Ghostface costume, but Angelina plays&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.560 --> 00:18:02.720
it off as her wanting to take props as souvenirs&nbsp;
now that the movie is shut down. But what if this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.720 --> 00:18:06.880
is actually her getting caught, and, being the&nbsp;
actress that she is, talking her way out of it?

00:18:06.880 --> 00:18:10.960
This could also be the reason that Roman decides&nbsp;
to kill her off. He probably figured that Sidney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:14.000
knew she was guilty, and it was better to&nbsp;
get rid of her before she had the chance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:14.000 --> 00:18:17.520
to be arrested and interrogated, where&nbsp;
she could possibly give them both away.

00:18:17.520 --> 00:18:19.680
He knew Sidney wouldn’t&nbsp;
accuse her immediately though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:19.680 --> 00:18:22.160
because she wouldn’t want a repeat&nbsp;
of Cotton Weary on her conscience,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:22.160 --> 00:18:26.720
who went to jail because he was wrongfully&nbsp;
accused by Sid when she wasn’t quite 100% sure.

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:30.960
So I think Angelina makes up an excuse, quickly&nbsp;
scurries away, and goes into the sound stage to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.960 --> 00:18:34.480
finish putting on the Ghostface costume. When&nbsp;
Sidney goes into the movie set version of her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.480 --> 00:18:37.840
room, there are clearly two killers here,&nbsp;
someone is pushing on the door, and then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.840 --> 00:18:41.840
3 seconds later Ghostface is all the way on the&nbsp;
other side of the room, behind her in the window.

00:18:41.840 --> 00:18:45.440
I think the one in the window is Angelina,&nbsp;
because the shoes we see during the struggle&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:45.440 --> 00:18:49.200
are the exact same as the ones that Sidney noticed&nbsp;
under the bathroom stall. When the lights go on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.200 --> 00:18:52.160
we get a better look at Angelina&nbsp;
wearing these shoes with a black…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.800 --> 00:18:56.560
Skirt? Uh... I don’t know the terminology&nbsp;
for this piece of clothing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:56.560 --> 00:19:00.400
but the loose black fabric could easily&nbsp;
help her blend into the Ghostface cloak.

00:19:00.400 --> 00:19:04.400
And the third sequence where she looks guilty&nbsp;
is Roman’s birthday party. Sure, they appear to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:09.120
be using the buddy system for safety, but Roman&nbsp;
and Angelina are each one-on-one with a victim.

00:19:09.120 --> 00:19:13.040
Dewey and Gale also poke around and find a&nbsp;
Ghostface mask, cell phone and voice changer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:16.480
in a closet. But these are not Roman’s&nbsp;
because he clearly has his own. I can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:19.600
understand having a backup costume,&nbsp;
but why have a second voice changer?

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:21.200
Angelina was supposed to be with Tyson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.200 --> 00:19:24.800
but she disappears, and Roman fakes his&nbsp;
death, just like Billy did. In hindsight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:28.560
he’s probably the one who taught Billy to&nbsp;
make that Hollywood level corn syrup mixture.

00:19:28.560 --> 00:19:32.320
During the big reveal, Roman even suggests&nbsp;
that he gave Billy pointers on being a killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:32.320 --> 00:19:34.800
such as having a partner to&nbsp;
sell out once you get caught.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:34.800 --> 00:19:37.840
This means Roman probably&nbsp;
also had a disposable partner.

00:19:37.840 --> 00:19:41.520
When we last see Angelina, she’s frantically&nbsp;
trying to leave the house. Could it be that at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:45.440
this point, she knows Roman is done with
her, and she’s now fearing for her life?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.440 --> 00:19:48.000
Or is this just part of the act?&nbsp;
We don’t actually see her die,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:48.000 --> 00:19:50.720
she just gets stabbed in the&nbsp;
shoulder, and dragged out of view.

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:53.920
It is awfully inconclusive for a&nbsp;
Scream character to go out like this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.920 --> 00:19:57.760
Maybe Scream 5 will feature Stu and&nbsp;
Angelina as the Ghostface killers.

00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:01.227
Personally, I don’t think there’s a ton of&nbsp;
evidence to suggest she’s alive, though it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:01.227 --> 00:20:05.360
is definitely a small possibility. But I am of&nbsp;
the belief that she was the Roman’s accomplice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:05.360 --> 00:20:09.760
and there’s a ton of evidence to support that. Let&nbsp;
me know your thoughts on Angelina in the comments.

